## Abstract

Static fire testing and pre-launch ground operations represent the highest-risk phases of liquid rocket development, historically generating 60–70% of catastrophic test failures through cascading fault modes that evade fixed deterministic thresholds. Contemporary safety architectures, relying on reactive interlocks and human-in-the-loop monitoring, exhibit critical limitations in detecting novel anomalies, quantifying decision uncertainty, and satisfying the increasingly stringent certification landscape of 2025–2026. This paper proposes a rigorously scoped, certifiable safety architecture that transitions from reactive thresholding to predictive, explainable, and autonomously verifiable safety management across four tightly integrated layers: (1) a Physics-Informed Reduced-Order Digital Twin (PINN-ROM) for real-time state estimation targeting sub-50 ms inference latency; (2) an unsupervised multimodal anomaly detection pipeline combining deep variational autoencoders with Extreme Value Theory (EVT) for threshold-free novel fault identification under modality incompleteness; (3) an N-version redundant decision architecture implementing Two-out-of-Three (2oo3) voting with deterministic hardware fallback; and (4) a graded Human-AI Teaming (HAT) protocol anchored in Explainable AI (XAI) and immutable cryptographic audit logging. The proposed framework is explicitly aligned with the regulatory environment effective in 2026: FAA 14 CFR Part 450 (fully mandatory from March 10, 2026), the EU AI Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689, with high-risk AI provisions active from August 2026), the NIST AI RMF Trustworthy AI Profile for Critical Infrastructure (April 2026 concept note), ISO 14620-1:2024, and EASA NPA 2025-07 on AI trustworthiness for aviation and space operations. Validation is structured across three phases — offline benchmarking, hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) emulation, and sub-scale static fire bench testing — targeting a TRL 5→6 transition by Year 4. The expected outcome is an industry-transferable safety architecture that demonstrably reduces catastrophic test failure rates, lowers false-positive abort rates below 10%, and establishes a replicable certification pathway for AI-augmented propulsion safety.

**Keywords:** Physics-informed neural networks, digital twin, anomaly detection, extreme value theory, N-version redundancy, human-AI teaming, liquid rocket engine, static fire safety, EU AI Act, explainable AI.

