## 1. Introduction

The validation of liquid rocket engines through static fire campaigns is an operationally irreplaceable and inherently hazardous process. Ground test sequences encompassing cryogenic propellant loading, ignition verification, full-thrust combustion monitoring, and autonomous shutdown logic expose test infrastructure, personnel, and vehicle hardware to compound risk profiles that have no reliable analogue in other engineering domains. Historical failure analyses consistently demonstrate that the majority of catastrophic test events originate not from a single sensor exceedance but from cascading, multi-modal fault sequences that propagate faster than deterministic interlock systems can respond and often manifest as previously unobserved fault topologies invisible to threshold-based detectors.

Current industrial practice in propulsion ground safety rests on three pillars that, while mature, are structurally inadequate for the complexity of modern liquid engine test campaigns: fixed-limit safety interlocks derived from worst-case design margins, human operator monitoring whose bandwidth and situational awareness are bounded by cognitive load constraints, and post-failure forensic analysis that yields knowledge too late to prevent the event. These limitations have been articulated across successive NASA, ESA, and ArianeGroup health management programs, most recently in the DiRETHMS, SAFEST, and ENLIGHTEN technical reports, which collectively advocate for edge-deployed continuous learning architectures capable of detecting fault precursors before threshold violation.

The academic and industrial landscape of 2025–2026 presents a convergence of enabling conditions that make a departure from purely reactive safety feasible: physics-informed machine learning capable of real-time inference at engineering-relevant latency; unsupervised anomaly detection that generalizes to novel fault modes without exhaustive labeled training data; edge computing platforms that support deterministic AI inference; and, critically, a maturing regulatory environment that now actively prescribes rather than merely permits AI-assisted safety decision-making in high-consequence aerospace operations.

Against this context, the present paper contributes the following. First, we formalize a four-layer certifiable safety architecture specifically scoped to liquid rocket static fire testing, moving beyond fragmented point solutions to an integrated system with well-defined interfaces, arbitration logic, and human oversight protocols. Second, we ground each architectural layer in the most current available literature and validate its design choices against measurable performance targets. Third, we construct a certification pathway explicitly mapped to the 2026 regulatory milieu, including the full-force application of FAA 14 CFR Part 450, the EU AI Act Annex III high-risk AI requirements, the NIST AI RMF Critical Infrastructure Profile, and EASA's inaugural AI trustworthiness rulemaking task. Fourth, we propose a phased validation strategy that advances the architecture from computational demonstration through hardware-in-the-loop emulation to sub-scale engine testing, achieving a documented TRL progression suitable for academic publication and industrial adoption.

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section 2 reviews the state of the art across the four constituent technical domains. Section 3 formalizes the problem statement and research questions. Section 4 describes the system architecture in detail. Section 5 presents the validation methodology and experimental design. Section 6 addresses the regulatory and certification framework. Section 7 discusses limitations and future extensions. Section 8 concludes.

