## 2. Related Work

### 2.1 Physics-Informed Reduced-Order Modeling for Propulsion Systems

The application of Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) to engineering system modeling has matured substantially since the foundational formulation of Raissi et al. (2019), which demonstrated that embedding governing differential equations into the neural network loss function regularizes learning and enforces physical consistency in regions of sparse data. The extension to digital twin scenarios has been systematically explored by Yang et al. (2024), who proposed multi-fidelity Data-Driven PINN (DD-PINN) frameworks achieving 42–62% prediction improvement over single-fidelity approaches while providing ensemble-based uncertainty quantification essential for safety-critical deployment.

Critically for propulsion applications, Xiao et al. (April 2026) demonstrated a RANS-based PINN incorporating the SST k-ω turbulence model for viscous compressible flows inside a rocket engine nozzle, achieving a 565× speedup per inference case relative to full CFD and reducing wall and throat region errors to below 7% through transfer learning. This result is foundational to the real-time digital twin layer proposed here: it confirms that PINN-based reduced-order inference can achieve the latency and accuracy targets required for online combustion monitoring without sacrificing the physical fidelity needed to interpret anomaly signatures.

For combustion instability specifically — one of the primary catastrophic failure modes in liquid rocket testing — Xu et al. developed a multi-fidelity framework integrating Proper Orthogonal Decomposition-based Reduced Order Models (POD-ROMs) trained on truncated domain simulations, demonstrating that training cost can be dramatically reduced without requiring full-geometry full-order model solutions. McQuarrie et al. further established data-driven ROM construction via regularized operator inference for single-injector combustion dynamics, blending scientific machine learning with physics-based structure preservation. These works collectively define the methodological space within which the PINN-ROM digital twin layer of our architecture operates.

For diesel engines, Nath et al. (2025) demonstrated a hybrid PINN-DeepONet digital twin framework that significantly reduces online computation cost while maintaining parameter identification accuracy, with a transfer learning component enabling rapid adaptation to new operating regimes — a capability directly applicable to the multi-test-condition variability inherent in static fire campaigns.

The simulation-to-reality gap remains the central unresolved challenge. Bayesian online calibration of ROM parameters using streaming telemetry has been proposed as the primary mitigation strategy, enabling the digital twin to continuously track the physical system's actual state rather than a fixed nominal model. The predictive output — mean state estimates with 95% credible intervals — directly feeds the anomaly detection layer with uncertainty-aware inputs rather than point predictions.

### 2.2 Multimodal Anomaly Detection for Liquid Rocket Engines

The literature on anomaly detection for liquid rocket engines (LREs) has progressed from univariate statistical process control and Gaussian Mixture Models, applied to Space Shuttle Main Engine (SSME) telemetry, through classical machine learning methods including PCA and one-class SVMs, to modern deep learning pipelines capable of multi-source data fusion.

The most directly relevant recent work is that of Feng et al. (IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks and Learning Systems, 2023), who proposed an unsupervised multimodal anomaly detection framework for LREs explicitly addressing the modality incompleteness problem. Their architecture handles intra-modality fusion, inter-modality fusion, and decision fusion within a unified framework of multiple deep autoencoders and a skip-connected AE, restoring missing modalities before secondary reconstruction and maximizing representational dissimilarity between normal and anomalous latent spaces. This directly addresses one of the most practically significant failure modes in propulsion test environments: sensor dropout, corrupted channels, or delayed transmission during critical phases. An independent validation on the International Journal of Aeronautical and Space Sciences (February 2026) has further confirmed the utility of multi-source fusion approaches for LRE anomaly detection in operationally realistic conditions.

The dual-control inference diffusion model (DIDM) of recent IEEE Xplore publication (2026) advances this further by applying channel conditioning and mask-prior controlled diffusion to reconstruct and infer from specified sensor subsets at specific moments, achieving finer-grained anomaly localization precision under the multi-sensor high-frequency monitoring conditions characteristic of static fire campaigns.

Knowledge distillation-optimized two-stage anomaly detection (ScienceDirect, 2023) and the retentive multimodal scale-variable convolutional autoencoder framework represent additional architectural options for data-limited conditions — directly relevant to the data scarcity problem inherent in rare failure mode detection.

For the threshold-free detection of novel fault modes, Extreme Value Theory provides the statistically principled foundation. EVT-based detection treats anomalies as extreme values arising from the tails of the reconstruction error distribution rather than requiring predefined thresholds or labeled anomaly examples. Guggilam et al. demonstrated a Bayesian EVT framework combining Dirichlet Process Mixture Models with pseudo-Gibbs inference for joint clustering and anomaly detection without thresholding parameters. Davis et al. showed that EVT-based detection rules are statistically superior to Gaussian-assumption methods on LSTM prediction errors across real-world datasets, with empirical support from transportation and aerospace applications. Multivariate EVT extensions enable anomaly clustering and visualization via angular measure estimation, providing interpretable structure to the anomaly space — an asset for XAI integration.

The progressive alerting taxonomy adopted in our architecture — Disturbance, Pre-Instability, Critical Failure Precursor — maps directly onto the confidence interval outputs of the EVT-scored reconstruction error pipeline, creating a three-tier actionable signal chain that avoids binary alarm states and enables graded operator response.

### 2.3 N-Version Redundancy and Deterministic Safety Architectures

N-version programming, originally proposed by Chen and Avizienis (1977) and extensively validated in avionics through DO-178C, provides formal diversity-based fault tolerance through independent implementation of equivalent functionality using different algorithms, data representations, and computational paths. Applied to AI-integrated safety architectures, N-version diversity extends beyond software redundancy to epistemic diversity: combining physics-based models that are provably stable under known conditions, data-driven models that generalize to novel patterns, and rule-based expert systems that encode validated operational experience.

The 2oo3 (Two-out-of-Three) voting mechanism provides the formal arbitration layer. AI recommendations are executed only when corroborated by at least one independent version, preventing single-model failure modes from propagating to actuation. Dynamic confidence gating supplements the static vote by weighting each version's contribution proportionally to its current output uncertainty, degrading gracefully as individual models encounter out-of-distribution inputs.

The AI certification literature of 2024–2025 highlights a persistent tension between the flexibility of learned models and the verifiability requirements of safety-critical standards. Sherifi (2024) notes explicitly that existing standards ARP-4754, DO-178C, and DO-254 fail to provide compliance pathways for AI-based systems, and that EASA and SAE International have been actively developing AI-specific standards since approximately 2022. The N-version architecture directly addresses this gap by confining AI contributions to a bounded advisory role within a deterministic arbitration structure, ensuring that no single AI failure mode can produce an uncontrolled safety consequence.

Common-cause failure mitigation demands physical as well as logical isolation: separate computation platforms, independent sensor suites fed from physically distinct measurement points, independent power rails, and an air-gapped mechanical kill switch immune to software state. This architecture maps directly onto the diverse hardware requirements articulated in RCC 319 for flight termination systems and NASA NPR 8705.2C human rating requirements.

### 2.4 Human-AI Teaming and Explainable AI in Safety-Critical Operations

The human factors dimension of AI-integrated safety systems has gained substantial academic and regulatory attention in 2025. Van Droogenbroeck et al. (HCII 2025, Springer) identify, from practitioner workshop data in the SESAR JARVIS project, that adoption of AI in safety-critical tasks requires navigating a fundamental dilemma: balancing process standards and certification with the transparency and trust requirements of genuine human-AI collaboration. They find that both the AI and the human operator must be able to communicate their motivations and reasoning — a requirement that directly motivates the XAI integration in our architecture.

The iScience review on human-AI interaction in safety-critical network infrastructures (August 2025) articulates a key distinction between interpretability and explainability in operational contexts, noting that tactical operators may not require algorithmic understanding, but policymakers and certification authorities require deeper insight into how specific configurations affect system-level safety. This asymmetry motivates the dual-level XAI output in our design: an operator-facing alert format optimized for rapid situational awareness under cognitive load, and a certification-facing attribution report providing full SHAP-based feature decomposition and physical causal mapping.

Toward a Holistic Framework for Human-AI Collaboration in Safety-Critical Systems (Springer, 2026) emphasizes that full automation is impractical for complex high-consequence tasks and introduces unintended negative consequences including operator skill degradation, automation bias, and loss of situational awareness during automation failures. The graded autonomy protocol in our architecture — Assist, Recommend, Act-with-Audit — is specifically designed to preserve meaningful human engagement across all confidence levels, avoiding the binary autonomous/manual dichotomy that generates the most severe human factors failures.

Research by Davis et al. on ad hoc human-machine teaming quantifies the benefits of XAI: novice operators show significant situational awareness improvement from XAI outputs, but are simultaneously susceptible to cognitive overhead when explanations exceed working memory capacity. This finding directly informs the constrained format of our XAI output: Risk Level | Confidence Interval | Dominant Features | Physical Attribution | Recommended Action — providing the minimum actionable information without inducing decision paralysis under time pressure.

