## 3. Problem Statement and Research Questions

**Core Problem:** How can a safety architecture for liquid rocket static fire testing reliably detect both known and novel fault precursors in real-time, quantify decision uncertainty, execute autonomous safety interventions, and satisfy the aerospace certification requirements that are legally binding from 2026, while maintaining meaningful human oversight?

**RQ1:** How can a Physics-Informed Reduced-Order Model integrated with Bayesian online calibration minimize the simulation-to-reality gap sufficiently to enable real-time digital twin inference below 50 ms latency for LOX/LCH₄ and LOX/RP-1 static fire conditions?

**RQ2:** How can an unsupervised multimodal anomaly detection pipeline integrating deep variational autoencoders with Extreme Value Theory detect novel fault modes without predefined thresholds while maintaining detection performance above 85% recall and below 10% false positive rate under conditions of up to two simultaneous sensor dropouts?

**RQ3:** What N-version redundant architecture and deterministic arbitration mechanism ensures fail-operational safety, mitigates common-cause failures between AI, physics-based, and rule-based decision paths, and satisfies the verifiability requirements of DO-178C adaptation guidelines and ECSS-Q-ST-40C?

**RQ4:** How can XAI attribution, multimodal cognitive load monitoring, and a graded autonomy protocol be structured to satisfy the EU AI Act Annex III transparency and human oversight requirements effective August 2026, the NIST AI RMF Critical Infrastructure Profile released April 2026, and the EASA NPA 2025-07 AI trustworthiness framework?

