## 4. System Architecture

### 4.1 Overview

The proposed architecture is organized into four functionally distinct but operationally coupled layers, processing sensor streams from test stand instrumentation through a cascade that produces graded safety actions and immutable audit records. The data flow is: raw sensor streams → PINN-ROM digital twin (state estimation + uncertainty) → multimodal anomaly detection pipeline (anomaly scores + EVT thresholds) → N-version arbitration engine (consensus decision) → graded human-AI teaming interface (operator action or autonomous intervention) → cryptographic audit logger (immutable record).

### 4.2 Layer 1: Physics-Informed Reduced-Order Digital Twin

The PINN-ROM digital twin replaces full-scale Computational Fluid Dynamics and Finite Element Analysis with a Proper Orthogonal Decomposition-based Reduced Order Model constrained by a Physics-Informed Neural Network that embeds the governing conservation laws — mass continuity, momentum transport, and energy balance — as soft constraints in the training loss function. For combustion chamber modeling, the RANS-based PINN formulation of Xiao et al. (2026), incorporating the SST k-ω turbulence closure, provides a validated template achieving sub-7% error across all flow variables with transfer learning reducing data requirements by approximately 50%.

The online calibration mechanism implements Bayesian parameter updating using streaming telemetry from high-frequency pressure transducers, fiber-optic temperature sensors, and structural strain gauges. At each inference cycle the posterior predictive distribution over system state is updated, producing a mean estimate and 95% credible interval that characterize the current nominal envelope. The credible interval width is itself a diagnostic signal: rapid posterior widening indicates that the physical system has departed from the ROM's trained operating regime, flagging potential off-nominal conditions independently of anomaly detection layer outputs.

Target performance specifications are: inference latency below 50 ms on edge GPU hardware (NVIDIA Jetson AGX class), state estimation RMSE below 5% versus physical sensors under nominal and off-nominal conditions, and continuous operation across cryogenic fueling, ignition transient, full-thrust combustion, and autonomous shutdown phases.

The digital twin output feeds Layer 2 not as a point prediction but as a structured uncertainty-aware state vector, enabling the anomaly detection layer to distinguish between genuine anomalies and regions of inherent model uncertainty — a distinction that conventional threshold systems cannot make.

### 4.3 Layer 2: Multimodal Unsupervised Anomaly Detection

The anomaly detection pipeline processes seven concurrent sensor modalities: high-frequency pressure transducers, tri-axial accelerometers, acoustic microphone arrays, multi-spectral high-speed imaging incorporating schlieren density gradient visualization, gas leak spectroscopy, and distributed fiber-optic strain and temperature sensing via Distributed Temperature Sensing (DTS) and Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS).

The algorithmic core adapts the unified multimodal autoencoder framework of Feng et al. (2023), extended with a Temporal Dependency Extraction Network that captures the sequential dynamics of fault propagation across modalities. Intra-modality fusion aggregates within-sensor temporal features; inter-modality fusion aligns cross-sensor representations; decision fusion combines modality-level anomaly scores through a Graph Neural Network with convex optimization-derived weighting that adapts to the current sensor availability state. Missing or degraded modalities are handled through probabilistic data association with fallback routing, preserving detection capability under the operational sensor degradation conditions validated by Feng et al. to recover from up to two simultaneous source failures.

Novel fault detection without predefined thresholds is implemented through Extreme Value Theory applied to the reconstruction error distribution. Following the theoretical framework of Guggilam et al. and the empirical validation of Davis et al., the Generalized Pareto Distribution is fitted to reconstruction error tails, establishing dynamic alert thresholds that adapt to evolving operating conditions without manual recalibration. This eliminates the primary operational failure mode of threshold-based systems — the accumulation of threshold violations from normal operational drift that generate false positives and erode operator trust.

The progressive alert taxonomy maps EVT-scored reconstruction errors to three operational states: Disturbance (sub-threshold tail probability, log and monitor), Pre-Instability (tail probability entering the GPD exceedance region, prepare abort procedures, activate dual authorization pre-arming), and Critical Failure Precursor (high-confidence EVT exceedance, transmit to N-version arbitration engine for immediate decision).

Synthetic data augmentation for rare failure modes uses physics-based simulation of fault injection scenarios — combustion oscillation triggering, propellant leak progression, and turbopump bearing degradation — to populate the training distribution in regions where historical operational data is absent, directly addressing the data scarcity problem identified as a primary limitation of pure data-driven approaches.

### 4.4 Layer 3: N-Version Redundant Safety Architecture

The arbitration engine integrates three independently implemented decision paths:

**V1 — Physics-Deterministic Version:** Analytical combustion stability boundaries derived from reduced-order acoustic eigenvalue analysis, combined with Fault-Tolerant Control laws operating on chamber pressure derivatives and structural vibration signatures. This version is provably stable, contains no learned component, and provides the ground truth for known failure envelope conditions.

**V2 — Data-Driven AI Version:** The anomaly probability outputs and risk forecasts from Layer 2, representing the architecture's capacity to detect novel and multi-modal fault patterns beyond the analytical stability envelope.

**V3 — Rule-Based Expert System:** Fault Tree Analysis and Event Tree Analysis-derived logic trees encoding validated operational experience, historical threshold mappings, and hardware interlock state monitoring. This version captures the institutional knowledge accumulated across previous test campaigns in an auditable, formally verifiable representation.

The 2oo3 voting mechanism requires agreement from at least two of the three versions before an AI-generated action recommendation is escalated to execution. Dynamic confidence gating weights each version's vote by its current output uncertainty, derived from the credible interval width of V1 predictions, the EVT tail probability of V2 scores, and the match quality of current system state to V3 rule preconditions.

The deterministic hardware watchdog operates independently of all software layers, monitoring raw sensor streams for hard physical limit violations — specifically, chamber pressure rate of change dP/dt exceeding a pre-computed structural integrity threshold, chamber wall temperature runaway beyond the ablative cooling limit, and turbopump speed exceedance beyond the mechanical burst margin. Upon any single hard limit violation the watchdog triggers immediate mechanical propellant valve closure through a physically hardwired electrical path immune to software latency, AI state, or network conditions. This provides the unconditional safety floor required by RCC 319 and NASA NPR 8705.2C.

Common-cause failure mitigation is implemented through: physically isolated computation platforms for V1, V2, and the watchdog; diverse sensor suites with independent measurement points for shared physical quantities; independent power rails with separate fault tolerance; and an air-gapped kill switch with mechanical backup accessible to the Range Safety Officer without dependence on any software system.

### 4.5 Layer 4: Graded Human-AI Teaming Protocol

The HAT protocol implements three autonomy levels that activate dynamically based on the current alert tier and operator cognitive load state:

**Assist Mode:** Active during nominal operations and Disturbance alerts. The AI highlights anomaly signatures and trend deviations on the operator display, generating SHAP-based feature attribution for any sub-threshold exceedance. The operator retains full decision authority and receives the AI's assessment as advisory information.

**Recommend Mode:** Active during Pre-Instability alerts. The AI generates a specific recommended action with confidence interval, physical attribution narrative, and counterfactual explanation (what parameter change would return the system to nominal). Operator confirmation via single biometric-verified acknowledgment is required before the AI recommendation is queued for execution.

**Act-with-Audit Mode:** Active when the N-version arbitration engine produces a 2oo3 consensus on a Critical Failure Precursor alert. The AI executes the consensus-selected safety action — staged shutdown, automated venting sequence, or full abort — while simultaneously generating an immutable cryptographic log entry and transmitting a real-time notification to the operator display. The human operator retains override authority at all times and can countermand an Act-with-Audit execution within a configurable time window before irreversible actuator states are reached.

Irreversible actions — propellant tank venting, full engine abort, and range safing — require biometric-verified dual operator authorization unless the deterministic watchdog has triggered, in which case the mechanical interlock executes independently.

Cognitive load monitoring fuses task-performance metrics (command response latency, procedural deviation events, command sequencing error rate) with physiological signals (heart rate variability, pupillometry, eye-tracking blink rate and fixation duration). The multimodal fusion approach, supported by the aviation cross-validation literature cited in the SESAR JARVIS project, provides superior cognitive state discrimination compared to single-metric fatigue estimation. When cognitive load monitoring indicates operator impairment above a validated threshold, the autonomy level automatically escalates one tier — not removing the operator from the loop, but increasing AI initiative while preserving human override.

The XAI output format at all autonomy levels conforms to the operator-facing template: **[Alert Level] | [Confidence Interval] | [Dominant Sensor Features] | [Physical Causal Attribution] | [Recommended Action]**. The certification-facing attribution report additionally includes full SHAP decomposition values for all input features, counterfactual scenario analysis, and temporal anomaly propagation trace — providing the transparency artifacts required under EU AI Act Article 13 and NIST AI RMF Measure function documentation.

