## 6. Regulatory and Certification Framework

### 6.1 Current Regulatory Landscape (2026)

The certification environment for AI-augmented aerospace safety systems has undergone substantive change in the period immediately preceding this research. FAA 14 CFR Part 450, governing commercial space launch and reentry license requirements, became mandatory for all license holders on March 10, 2026. Part 450 Section 450.108 defines flight safety system requirements; Section 450.145 specifies requirements for highly reliable flight safety systems; and Section 450.143 governs safety-critical systems more broadly. The RCC 319 Flight Termination System Safety Requirements document is accepted as a Means of Compliance for 450.145. The proposed architecture's N-version redundant structure and deterministic watchdog are designed to satisfy the 450.143/450.145 requirements for ground test safety systems with equivalent rigor.

The EU AI Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689), in force from August 1, 2024, became fully applicable to high-risk AI systems on August 2, 2026, with an extended transition period for AI embedded in regulated products until August 2, 2028. Annex III classifies AI systems used in the management and operation of critical infrastructure as high-risk, which includes propulsion test facility AI safety systems. Applicable requirements include technical documentation, logging and auditability, transparency and operator information obligations, human oversight provisions, and robustness, accuracy, and cybersecurity requirements. The AI omnibus simplification, adopted November 19, 2025 with political agreement reached May 7, 2026, clarifies implementation requirements and reduces administrative burden for smaller operators without relaxing safety substantive obligations. Commission guidelines for high-risk AI classification were released for consultation on May 19, 2026.

EASA NPA 2025-07, the first regulatory proposal on AI for aviation under Rulemaking Task RMT.0742, provides technical guidance on AI trustworthiness aligned with EU AI Act Annex III. The NPA distinguishes AI-based assistance (Level 1) from Human-AI teaming (Level 2) — directly mapping to our Recommend and Act-with-Audit autonomy modes respectively — and addresses AI assurance, human factors, and ethics. A second NPA is planned for 2026 to extend this generic framework to specific aviation and space domains.

The NIST AI Risk Management Framework has evolved in the direction of sector-specific operational guidance. On April 7, 2026, NIST released a concept note for an AI RMF Profile on Trustworthy AI in Critical Infrastructure, guiding operators toward specific risk management practices for AI-enabled capabilities. NIST AI 100-5 addresses agentic AI systems; NIST AI 100-2 addresses adversarial machine learning — both directly relevant to the robustness requirements of our architecture. The AI Agent Standards Initiative, announced by NIST CAISI in February 2026, is developing voluntary guidelines covering identity and authorization, security and risk management, and monitoring and logging, with an AI Agent Interoperability Profile planned for Q4 2026.

### 6.2 Claim-Argument-Evidence Safety Case Structure

The certification deliverable is a formal safety case structured on the Claim-Argument-Evidence (CAE) pattern, aligned with ECSS-Q-ST-40C software product assurance requirements and ISO 14620-1:2024 space systems safety management.

The top-level claim is: *The PINN-ROM/Multimodal-AD/N-Version/HAT safety architecture provides a level of safety for LOX/LCH₄ and LOX/RP-1 static fire test operations that is at least equivalent to conventional deterministic interlock systems, while detecting a broader class of fault precursors with quantified uncertainty and documented human oversight.*

Supporting arguments are organized into five sub-claims: (1) the PINN-ROM digital twin accurately represents physical system state within documented uncertainty bounds; (2) the multimodal anomaly detection pipeline detects known and novel fault precursors above the specified recall target; (3) the N-version architecture provides fault-tolerant arbitration with no single point of AI failure propagating to uncontrolled actuation; (4) the graded HAT protocol preserves meaningful human oversight in compliance with EU AI Act Article 14; (5) the immutable audit logging system provides post-event reconstruction capability satisfying EU AI Act Article 12 and FAA Part 450 documentation requirements.

Evidence for each sub-claim is drawn from: Phase 1–3 validation test reports, adversarial testing campaign results, independent third-party safety review dossier, HIL deterministic latency validation records, and cryptographically timestamped decision logs from static fire bench tests. The regulatory mapping matrix cross-references each architectural element to specific clauses of EU AI Act, NIST AI RMF, ECSS-Q-ST-40C, ISO 14620-1:2024, FAA 14 CFR Part 450, and EASA NPA 2025-07.

