## 7. Discussion

### 7.1 Anticipated Criticisms and Responses

**On AI reliability in safety decisions:** The architecture explicitly constrains AI contribution to Version 2 of a three-version ensemble. No AI output alone can trigger a safety actuation; 2oo3 consensus with at least one non-AI version is required. The deterministic watchdog provides an AI-independent safety floor. This structural argument is the primary response to safety authority skepticism regarding AI decision autonomy.

**On real-time computational feasibility:** The RANS-PINN result of Xiao et al. (2026) demonstrates 565× speedup over full CFD with sub-7% error — directly validating the computational feasibility of PINN-ROM at the target latency. Full CFD/FEA is retained exclusively for offline design-phase validation.

**On false-positive rates:** EVT-based dynamic thresholding replaces fixed limits, adapting to the current operating regime. Progressive alerting provides Disturbance → Pre-Instability → Critical escalation paths rather than binary alarm states, enabling controlled operator response without automated abort on low-confidence signals. The 10% FPR target is validated in Phase 1 and confirmed through Phase 3 abort rate analysis.

**On data scarcity for rare failure modes:** Physics-based synthetic data generation, domain adaptation from related propulsion test databases, and the threshold-free EVT detection paradigm collectively address this limitation. EVT requires only nominal operational data for tail distribution fitting; explicit anomaly examples are not required for threshold establishment.

**On the 2026 regulatory timeline:** The architecture is designed against the regulatory state that is currently in force or will be in force by August 2026, with explicit attention to the transition periods and extended deadlines specified in the AI omnibus. The certification pathway accounts for the 2028 extended deadline for AI embedded in regulated products, providing a realistic timeline for industrial adoption.

### 7.2 Scope and Generalizability

The architecture is validated on LOX/LCH₄ and LOX/RP-1 propellant combinations. Hypergolic systems present different sensor modality requirements (toxic gas speciation replaces standard combustion imaging), and hybrid systems introduce regression rate monitoring as an additional fusion input. Both extensions require propellant-specific recalibration of anomaly baselines and chemistry-aware sensor weighting but do not necessitate architectural redesign.

The scope boundary at ground test operations — excluding flight safety, orbital dynamics, and meteorological coupling — is a deliberate research scoping decision rather than an architectural limitation. The digital thread continuity from design-phase simulation through ground test digital twin to flight represents a natural extension pathway for post-doctoral work.

