## 8. Conclusion

This paper has presented a four-layer certifiable safety architecture for liquid rocket static fire testing that integrates physics-informed reduced-order digital twins, EVT-augmented multimodal anomaly detection, N-version redundant arbitration, and graded human-AI teaming into a unified framework explicitly designed for the regulatory environment in force from 2026. Each architectural layer is grounded in peer-reviewed literature published through April 2026 and validated against performance targets derived from operational requirements.

The central contribution is architectural and regulatory coherence: moving beyond the fragmented point solutions that characterize current AI-in-propulsion-safety research toward an integrated system with well-defined interfaces, deterministic fallback guarantees, human oversight protocols calibrated to current standards, and a certifiable audit trail. The framework does not position AI as a replacement for deterministic safety engineering — it positions AI as a complementary detection capability whose outputs are constrained, arbitrated, and documented within a structure that satisfies the trust and verifiability requirements that aerospace certification authorities are now actively codifying.

The expected contribution to the field is a TRL 5→6 validated safety architecture, an open benchmark dataset, a certification-ready CAE safety case template, and a regulatory mapping matrix that operationalizes the EU AI Act, NIST AI RMF Critical Infrastructure Profile, EASA NPA 2025-07, and FAA 14 CFR Part 450 requirements for propulsion test facility AI deployment. Together, these outputs aim to reduce the gap between AI capability and AI deployment in the safety-critical aerospace environments where its benefits are most consequential.


