### D.3 Hardware-in-the-Loop Testbed Configuration

The HIL testbed is organized into four physically separated racks interconnected via deterministic switched Ethernet (IEEE 802.1 Time-Sensitive Networking):

**Rack 1 — Sensor Simulation:** Real-time signal generators reproducing the full sensor suite at specified sampling rates. Supports: nominal signal playback from real telemetry archives, synthetic fault injection (amplitude anomaly, drift, spike, freeze, noise floor elevation), missing channel simulation (physical disconnection or software masking), and spoofed signal injection via man-in-the-middle on the communication bus.

**Rack 2 — AI Compute (Under Test):** Production Jetson AGX Orin platform running the full PINN-ROM + DVAE-TDEN + GNN + arbitration software stack under VxWorks 7. Instrumented with hardware performance counters monitoring CPU/GPU utilization, memory bandwidth, and inference latency at 1 ms resolution.

**Rack 3 — Watchdog and Interlocks:** Intel Agilex FPGA platform implementing the deterministic watchdog logic, actuator command relay simulation, and hardware interlock state machine. Instrumented for response time measurement with 10 ns resolution via oscilloscope trigger.

**Rack 4 — Operator Interface and Audit:** Simulation of the operator workstation display, cognitive load monitoring sensor inputs (via pre-recorded physiological signal playback), dual-authorization biometric emulator, and audit log receiver with cryptographic verification.

**Network Jitter Injection:** A programmable network impairment emulator introduces variable latency (0–50 ms, configurable distribution), packet loss (0–5%), and burst delay (0–200 ms) on inter-rack communication paths to stress-test the pipeline's real-time performance under realistic network degradation.

