## Appendix G: Glossary

**AFTS / AFTU:** Autonomous Flight Termination System / Unit — onboard system performing automated flight safety decisions without ground controller input.

**CAE:** Claim-Argument-Evidence — structured safety case pattern used in ECSS, UK MoD DEF STAN 00-56, and aerospace certification.

**CFR Part 450:** Code of Federal Regulations, Title 14, Part 450 — FAA commercial space launch and reentry licensing regulation, fully mandatory from March 10, 2026.

**DAS:** Distributed Acoustic Sensing — fiber-optic technique measuring acoustic and vibrational fields along the fiber length via Rayleigh backscattering.

**DD-PINN:** Data-Driven Physics-Informed Neural Network — PINN variant incorporating available measurement data in addition to physics constraints.

**DFA:** Deterministic Finite Automaton — formal computational model used here to specify the alert state machine.

**DTS:** Distributed Temperature Sensing — fiber-optic temperature measurement using Raman backscattering.

**DVAE:** Deep Variational Autoencoder — generative model learning a probabilistic latent representation for anomaly detection via reconstruction error.

**EASA NPA 2025-07:** European Union Aviation Safety Agency Notice of Proposed Amendment 2025-07 — first regulatory proposal on AI trustworthiness for aviation, aligned with EU AI Act Annex III.

**ECE:** Expected Calibration Error — metric quantifying the agreement between predicted probabilities and empirical frequencies across confidence bins.

**ELBO:** Evidence Lower Bound — variational inference objective maximized in VAE training as a tractable surrogate for the intractable marginal log-likelihood.

**EU AI Act:** Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — the European Union's comprehensive legal framework governing AI development and deployment, in force August 1, 2024, high-risk AI provisions applicable August 2, 2026.

**EVT:** Extreme Value Theory — statistical framework characterizing the behavior of distribution tails, enabling principled threshold-free anomaly detection.

**FBG:** Fiber Bragg Grating — wavelength-encoded fiber optic sensing element for strain and temperature measurement.

**GNN:** Graph Neural Network — deep learning architecture operating on graph-structured data, used here for inter-modality sensor fusion.

**GPD:** Generalized Pareto Distribution — limiting distribution for peaks-over-threshold exceedances under EVT, parameterized by shape ξ and scale σ.

**HAT:** Human-AI Teaming — collaborative operational mode where human operators and AI systems share situational awareness, decision support, and action authority.

**HIL:** Hardware-in-the-Loop — validation methodology interfacing real hardware with simulated plant models, testing software under realistic I/O conditions.

**HMAC:** Hash-based Message Authentication Code — cryptographic construct for verifying message integrity and authenticity, used in immutable audit logging.

**LOX:** Liquid Oxygen — cryogenic oxidizer used in LOX/LCH₄ (methane) and LOX/RP-1 (kerosene) bipropellant systems.

**MLE:** Maximum Likelihood Estimation — statistical parameter estimation method minimizing the negative log-likelihood of observed data.

**NASA-TLX:** NASA Task Load Index — validated multidimensional subjective workload assessment instrument.

**NTK:** Neural Tangent Kernel — theoretical framework describing neural network training dynamics in the infinite-width limit, used here to derive adaptive loss weighting.

**PINN:** Physics-Informed Neural Network — neural network incorporating governing physical equations as additional loss terms during training.

**POD:** Proper Orthogonal Decomposition — optimal linear basis extraction from an ensemble of snapshots, also known as Principal Component Analysis in the discrete setting.

**POT:** Peaks-Over-Threshold — EVT method fitting the GPD to exceedances above a baseline threshold u.

**RANS:** Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes — turbulence modeling approach averaging the Navier-Stokes equations over turbulent fluctuations, requiring a closure model.

**ROM:** Reduced-Order Model — computationally efficient surrogate for a high-fidelity model, operating in a low-dimensional subspace of the full state space.

**RMSSD:** Root Mean Square of Successive Differences — HRV metric sensitive to parasympathetic (rest/digest) nervous system activity; decreases under cognitive load.

**SHAP:** SHapley Additive exPlanations — game-theoretic framework attributing model output contributions to individual input features, used for XAI.

**SST k-ω:** Shear Stress Transport k-ω — two-equation turbulence model blending k-ε behavior in the freestream with k-ω behavior near walls.

**TDEN:** Temporal Dependency Extraction Network — multi-head temporal attention module extracting sequential feature dependencies from each sensor modality.

**TRL:** Technology Readiness Level — NASA/ESA scale (1–9) characterizing the maturity of a technology from basic principles (TRL 1) to full operational deployment (TRL 9).

**UKF:** Unscented Kalman Filter — derivative-free Bayesian state estimation algorithm propagating sigma points through nonlinear system models.

**XAI:** Explainable Artificial Intelligence — methods and techniques producing human-interpretable justifications for AI model outputs.

**2oo3:** Two-out-of-Three — voting majority logic requiring agreement from at least two of three independent systems before a decision is executed.



